course: dictionary
lesson: 6828
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word ottoman can name a great empire, describe things from that empire, or name a piece of furniture. «The Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople in 1453.» Here, Ottoman with a capital O names an empire and its rulers, so it must be about history.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Is it talking about kings, cities and battles, or about a room, a seat and somewhere to rest your feet? «Sam put his feet up on the ottoman by the fire.» Feet, a fire and a room tell you this is a low, padded seat.
explain: Always test your meaning. Swap it into the sentence and check that it still makes sense. If the empire meaning does not fit a sentence about resting your feet, the furniture meaning is the right one.
fact: The Ottoman Empire was a large, long-lasting empire ruled by Turkish sultans from Istanbul.
fact: The Ottomans ruled lands in Turkey, south-east Europe, the Middle East and North Africa.
fact: Ottoman with a capital O is a name for the empire, its rulers and its people.
fact: An ottoman with a small o is a low, padded seat or footstool, often with a lid and storage space inside.
fact: When you meet an unknown word, look at its context first, then check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence.
exercise: «The Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople in 1453.» What does Ottoman mean here? | A hot drink served in busy cafés | A long river in Africa | *The Ottoman Empire and its rulers | A soft footstool for resting your feet
exercise: «Sam put his feet up on the ottoman by the fire.» What is an ottoman in this sentence? | A ruler from Istanbul | *A low, padded seat or footstool | A type of cloud | A city in south-east Europe
exercise: «Istanbul was Constantinople, the Byzantine capital, which the Ottomans conquered in 1453.» Find and copy the word that tells you the Ottomans won the city by force. | *conquered | Istanbul | Byzantine | capital
test: «The Ottomans ruled a huge empire from Istanbul.» Here Ottomans means | a kind of ship used on rivers | *the rulers and people of that empire | a mountain in Turkey | a row of soft chairs in a living room
test: Which sentence uses Ottoman to mean "of or about that empire"? | «She bought a new ottoman with a lid for her bedroom.» | *«Ottoman coffee houses were busy places in the city.» | «The ottoman had a soft red cover and a hidden drawer.» | «The ottoman was too low for him to sit on comfortably.»
exam: Jayden says ottoman always means a footstool. Is he right? | No, it only means a long river | Yes, because the word always has a capital letter | Yes, it only ever means a seat in a living room | *No, it can also name an empire, so the context decides
